::CW_U05_UA_Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q01
Draft problem: A peer says the ending is unclear because the reader cannot tell why the character leaves. What is the best next step?{
~A. Change every character name.#Incorrect. Names do not address motivation.
=B. Add or revise a moment that shows the character's reason for leaving.#Correct. This responds directly to the feedback.
~C. Ignore the feedback because all endings are confusing.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered.
~D. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation will not clarify motivation.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q02
Workshop context: Which feedback is most useful?{
~A. I do not like stories like this.#Incorrect. This is personal preference.
~B. It is good.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. Change everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad.
=D. The repeated hallway image builds tension; consider clarifying what the character fears at the end.#Correct. This names a strength and a focused suggestion.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q03
Format context: A script has dialogue but no character labels. What should be revised?{
=A. Add clear speaker labels and useful stage directions.#Correct. This supports script readability.
~B. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue is central to script form.
~C. Add random stanza breaks.#Incorrect. Stanza breaks do not solve script speaker clarity.
~D. Ignore formatting.#Incorrect. Format matters.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q04
Which answer best explains author craft reasoning?{
~A. I like my poem.#Incorrect. This is opinion.
~B. The poem has words.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=C. I placed the final image on its own line so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a craft choice and reader effect.
~D. I wrote it quickly.#Incorrect. Speed is not craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q05
Which piece is most publication-ready?{
=A. A revised poem with purposeful line breaks, checked conventions, clear title, and appropriate content.#Correct. This includes revision, format, conventions, and audience readiness.
~B. A rough draft with no title.#Incorrect. It lacks readiness checks.
~C. A script with no speaker labels.#Incorrect. This has a format issue.
~D. A story with random spacing and no review.#Incorrect. Random spacing and no review weaken readiness.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q06
Feedback says a paragraph is confusing because two speakers are in one paragraph. Which revision best fits?{
~A. Add more unrelated setting.#Incorrect. The issue is speaker clarity.
~B. Change the title only.#Incorrect. The title does not solve dialogue confusion.
~C. Delete all dialogue.#Incorrect. A targeted revision is better.
=D. Start a new paragraph when the speaker changes and check dialogue tags.#Correct. This improves clarity and format.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q07
Which answer should be eliminated first?{
~A. Consider feedback and protect the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. This is strong.
=B. Accept every suggestion automatically.#Correct. Automatic acceptance can damage purpose and voice.
~C. Explain reader effect.#Incorrect. Reader effect is important.
~D. Check format before publication.#Incorrect. Format checks are important.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q08
A portfolio note should include what?{
~A. Only "I like it."#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~B. Private personal information.#Incorrect. That is inappropriate.
=C. Selection reason, revision evidence, and final readiness explanation.#Correct. This supports portfolio readiness.
~D. A list of random colors.#Incorrect. That does not explain writing growth.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q09
Which is the best author presentation opening?{
~A. My story has stuff in it.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. I will tell the whole plot first.#Incorrect. This over-summarizes.
~C. I do not know why I wrote it.#Incorrect. This lacks purpose.
=D. "Open Door" is a short story meant to show a character deciding whether to trust someone again.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q10
Which revision most directly improves reader effect?{
=A. Replacing "I was sad" with an image of the last bus passing without stopping.#Correct. This shows emotion through image.
~B. Changing the font randomly.#Incorrect. This does not improve the writing itself.
~C. Deleting the title for no reason.#Incorrect. That may weaken reader preparation.
~D. Ignoring feedback about clarity.#Incorrect. That does not improve reader effect.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q11
What is active listening in workshop?{
~A. Interrupting before the writer finishes.#Incorrect. That blocks listening.
=B. Restating the writer's purpose before giving feedback.#Correct. This shows understanding before response.
~C. Giving only personal preference.#Incorrect. Feedback should be craft-focused.
~D. Ignoring the writer's goal.#Incorrect. The goal matters.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q12
Which is a strong publication decision?{
~A. Post another student's work without permission.#Incorrect. That violates authorship.
=B. Use a school-appropriate excerpt and remove private identifying details.#Correct. This supports safe online publication.
~C. Publish a rough draft without review.#Incorrect. Readiness checks are needed.
~D. Mock peer writing in a comment.#Incorrect. Publication must be respectful.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q13
Which statement best separates revision and polishing?{
=A. Revision improves meaning or structure; polishing checks final details.#Correct. This separates major improvement from final cleanup.
~B. Polishing fixes purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose is a revision issue.
~C. Revision means random changes.#Incorrect. Revision is purposeful.
~D. Polishing should happen before reading the draft.#Incorrect. Polishing comes after review.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q14
A poem uses random line breaks that make the central image hard to read. What should the writer do?{
~A. Keep the random breaks because all poems must be confusing.#Incorrect. Format should support meaning.
~B. Delete every image.#Incorrect. The issue is line breaks.
~C. Ignore the reader.#Incorrect. Reader understanding matters.
=D. Revise line breaks so the image and pauses support the intended effect.#Correct. This improves format-specific quality.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q15
Which response best explains a revision decision?{
~A. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This does not explain improvement.
~B. I changed random words.#Incorrect. Random change is not revision reasoning.
=C. I shortened the ending because feedback showed the first version explained too much.#Correct. This connects feedback, change, and improvement.
~D. I did not revise anything.#Incorrect. This does not explain revision.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q16
Which feedback is least helpful?{
~A. The opening image creates suspense.#Incorrect. This names a strength.
=B. Make it better.#Correct. This is too vague to guide revision.
~C. What does the character want in the final scene?#Incorrect. This is a useful question.
~D. Consider adding one sound before the reveal.#Incorrect. This is a focused suggestion.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q17
Which checklist best fits Unit 5?{
~A. Type, post, ignore feedback.#Incorrect. This skips the workflow.
~B. Decorate randomly and submit.#Incorrect. Random decoration is not readiness.
~C. Summarize the plot only.#Incorrect. Unit 5 includes more than summary.
=D. Understand purpose, consider feedback, revise, check format and conventions, prepare readers, explain author reasoning.#Correct. This synthesizes the unit.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q18
A writer's presentation retells every event but gives no craft reasoning. What is the best correction?{
=A. Focus on purpose, two craft choices, and one revision decision.#Correct. This matches author presentation expectations.
~B. Add more plot summary.#Incorrect. Summary is already the issue.
~C. Remove the creative work entirely.#Incorrect. The work should remain central.
~D. Avoid mentioning craft.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q19
Which answer best handles online collaborative feedback?{
~A. Let reviewers erase the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. Purpose should be protected.
=B. Consider suggestions, accept what improves the piece, and reject what conflicts with purpose.#Correct. This is thoughtful collaboration.
~C. Reject every comment automatically.#Incorrect. Some feedback may help.
~D. Publish without review.#Incorrect. Review supports readiness.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q20
Which convention issue should be fixed before publication?{
=A. Dialogue punctuation that makes speakers hard to follow.#Correct. This affects clarity.
~B. A purposeful line break in a poem.#Incorrect. Purposeful line breaks may be effective.
~C. A strong title.#Incorrect. A strong title should remain.
~D. A clear author note.#Incorrect. This supports publication.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q21
Which selection rationale is strongest?{
=A. I selected this story because revision from feedback clarified the character's decision and made the ending stronger.#Correct. This connects selection to growth.
~B. I selected this because it was first in my folder.#Incorrect. That is not a quality reason.
~C. I selected this because I did not read the others.#Incorrect. That is not purposeful.
~D. I selected this because it has no title.#Incorrect. Missing title is not a strength.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q22
Which answer best identifies reader effect?{
~A. The writer changed something.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=B. The shorter sentence slows the reader at the final decision.#Correct. This explains effect.
~C. The piece exists.#Incorrect. This does not identify effect.
~D. The author used a computer.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q23
Which is the strongest response to vague feedback?{
=A. Ask for the specific place where the reader became confused.#Correct. This turns vague feedback into useful information.
~B. Accept it without question.#Incorrect. Vague feedback needs clarification.
~C. Delete the draft.#Incorrect. That is extreme.
~D. Ignore all future feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback can help.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q24
Which author note is best for publication?{
~A. This piece is about everything and nothing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B. This poem uses the bus stop image to show waiting; I revised the final line so the speaker's choice is clearer.#Correct. This gives context and revision insight.
~C. Do not read this.#Incorrect. This does not prepare readers.
~D. I will explain every word.#Incorrect. This overexplains.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q25
Which response shows Unit 5 mastery?{
=A. I can use feedback to revise, check format and conventions, prepare work for publication, and explain craft choices to an audience.#Correct. This summarizes the unit's major skills.
~B. I can type a rough draft and stop.#Incorrect. This skips the unit skills.
~C. I can ignore readers and feedback.#Incorrect. Reader effect and feedback matter.
~D. I can choose answers randomly.#Incorrect. Mastery requires purposeful reasoning.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q26::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q26
Draft excerpt: I walked into the room. It was weird. Something felt different. Feedback: I cannot picture the room. Which revision goal is best?{
~A. Change every sentence because the draft is bad.#Incorrect. Revision should be targeted, not random.
~B. Delete the whole draft and start over.#Incorrect. The feedback names a specific issue, so a targeted revision is better.
=C. Add one specific detail that helps readers picture the room.#Correct. The feedback says the room is hard to picture, so a targeted setting detail directly addresses the issue.
~D. Add a new character with no connection to the room.#Incorrect. That does not solve the setting problem.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q27::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q27
Draft excerpt: The poem is about missing my old neighborhood. Feedback: Add one concrete image from the neighborhood. Which revision best uses the feedback?{
=A. Add the image of the corner store sign flickering after rain.#Correct. This adds a concrete neighborhood image, which is exactly what the feedback requests.
~B. Add a sentence saying the poem is emotional.#Incorrect. That tells feeling but does not add a concrete image.
~C. Remove all details about the neighborhood.#Incorrect. That works against the feedback.
~D. Change the poem into a research report.#Incorrect. The feedback asks for an image, not a new genre.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q28::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q28
Feedback: Make it better. How should this feedback be classified?{
~A. Specific and actionable.#Incorrect. It does not identify what needs improvement.
=B. Vague and not actionable.#Correct. "Make it better" does not tell the writer what to revise or why.
~C. A clear revision goal.#Incorrect. It is too general to be a goal.
~D. A convention correction.#Incorrect. It does not mention grammar, punctuation, or spelling.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q29::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q29
Draft sentence: Mara handed Lena the box after she dropped it, and it made her upset. Which revision is clearest?{
~A. She handed it over after it happened and it was upsetting.#Incorrect. This keeps the pronoun confusion.
~B. The box was there and it was a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. After Maya opened the box, she handed the old photograph to Lena.#Correct. This revision names the people and object clearly so the reader knows who did what.
~D. They did it and then it happened.#Incorrect. This is even less clear.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q30::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.03
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q30
Draft: The rain stopped. I found the letter. The house was quiet. Which revision improves cohesion?{
=A. When the rain stopped, I found the letter on the quiet porch.#Correct. This combines the ideas smoothly and shows the relationship between events.
~B. The rain stopped. I found the letter. The house was quiet.#Incorrect. This repeats the choppy original.
~C. Letter quiet rain house found.#Incorrect. This removes sentence clarity.
~D. I found quiet because rain letter.#Incorrect. This is not coherent.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q31::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q31
Workshop context: A writer says, "I want this scene to feel tense but still clear." Which response best shows active listening?{
~A. I like funny scenes better.#Incorrect. This focuses on personal preference instead of the writer's purpose.
~B. You should change the whole scene.#Incorrect. This gives a broad command before showing that the purpose was understood.
=C. You want the scene to create tension while the reader can still follow what is happening.#Correct. This restates the writer's purpose clearly before giving feedback.
~D. The scene is probably finished already.#Incorrect. This avoids listening for the writer's revision goal.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q32::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q32
Workshop context: A peer draft uses the line, "The porch light held its breath." Which feedback best names a craft strength?{
=A. The personification of the porch light creates a quiet, tense mood.#Correct. This names the craft move and explains its effect.
~B. I like porch lights.#Incorrect. This is personal preference and does not explain the craft strength.
~C. You should write about a different house.#Incorrect. This changes the topic without explaining what is working.
~D. This is good.#Incorrect. This is too general to help the writer revise.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q33::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q33
Draft excerpt: I waited outside the rehearsal room. The music stopped. I raised my hand to knock, then lowered it. The writer wants a poem that emphasizes hesitation. Which format choice best fits?{
~A. Put the entire draft in one long line.#Incorrect. One long line may reduce the pause that shows hesitation.
~B. Add random spaces between every word.#Incorrect. Random spacing distracts the reader and does not clearly support meaning.
=C. Use line breaks around "raised my hand" and "lowered it" so the reader pauses with the speaker.#Correct. The format choice supports pacing and meaning.
~D. Remove all punctuation and format choices.#Incorrect. Removing all control can make the piece harder to read.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q34::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.02
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q34
Draft excerpt: "Come in" Mara said but she did not open the door. Which prose-story revision best improves publication quality?{
=A. "Come in," Mara said, but she did not open the door.#Correct. The punctuation and sentence control make the dialogue clear.
~B. come in mara said but she did not open the door#Incorrect. This removes punctuation and capitalization, which weakens readability.
~C. COME IN MARA SAID BUT SHE DID NOT OPEN THE DOOR#Incorrect. All capitals do not create clear prose formatting.
~D. Come in. Mara. Door. Said.#Incorrect. This fragments the sentence and makes meaning less clear.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q35::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q35
Publication context: A student finishes typing a rough draft and wants to place it in the class online publication space immediately. What should happen first?{
~A. Post it immediately because typing means it is finished.#Incorrect. A typed draft still needs revision, format, and readiness checks.
~B. Delete the draft and start over.#Incorrect. The draft may be useful; it needs a publication workflow.
=C. Review purpose, feedback, format, audience appropriateness, and final conventions before publication.#Correct. Publication-ready work requires more than simply posting a draft.
~D. Change the title only and ignore the rest.#Incorrect. A title is important, but it is not the only publication check.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q36::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.PUB.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q36
Collaborative publication context: Which role checks whether line breaks, paragraph breaks, title, and spacing fit the creative form?{
=A. Format checker.#Correct. The format checker reviews whether the piece's visual and structural choices support the form.
~B. Weather reporter.#Incorrect. This is not a publication role.
~C. Unrelated reader.#Incorrect. The role needs a specific publication responsibility.
~D. Person who changes the writer's purpose.#Incorrect. Collaboration should not erase the writer's purpose.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q37::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q37
Author presentation context: Which opening is strongest?{
~A. My story has a driveway, rain, and an umbrella, and then things happen.#Incorrect. This mostly summarizes events without purpose.
~B. I do not know what my piece is about.#Incorrect. The author needs to frame the work clearly.
=C. "After the Rain" is a short prose piece meant to show a character who is safe but still unsure how to move forward.#Correct. This names title, form, and purpose.
~D. The audience should figure out everything with no help.#Incorrect. An author presentation should provide a useful entry point.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q38::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q38
Which sentence best explains craft reasoning?{
=A. I repeated the umbrella image so the reader could see the character holding onto protection even after the rain stopped.#Correct. This names the craft choice and explains reader effect.
~B. I used an umbrella because umbrellas exist.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft purpose.
~C. The story has words and sentences.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. I made choices because writing needs choices.#Incorrect. This does not explain a specific reader effect.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q39::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q39
Portfolio context: A writer chooses a story only because it is the shortest draft. What is the best correction?{
~A. Choose it automatically because short always means portfolio-ready.#Incorrect. Length does not prove readiness.
~B. Choose the first draft found and skip revision.#Incorrect. A portfolio piece needs a reason and revision evidence.
=C. Choose a piece because it shows growth, strong craft, or clear publication potential.#Correct. Portfolio selection should be purposeful.
~D. Choose no piece because portfolios cannot include creative work.#Incorrect. A portfolio is built from selected creative work.
}

::CW_U05_UA_Q40::
MLA Standard: MLA.CW.COM.01
Question ID: CW_U05_UA_Q40
A draft has a confusing ending and several spelling errors. What should the writer address first?{
=A. Revise the ending so the reader understands the character's decision.#Correct. Meaning and purpose should be fixed before final polishing.
~B. Correct spelling only and ignore the ending.#Incorrect. Polishing does not solve the larger meaning problem.
~C. Add random formatting.#Incorrect. Random formatting does not clarify the ending.
~D. Write the portfolio note before revising.#Incorrect. The note should reflect the final revised piece.
}
